+## Coverage and compatibility
+
+The current `0107-benefit-claim-reviews` contract contains 51 durable tables.
+It includes `marketing_webhook_events`, which preserves callback deduplication
+and opt-out event history, as well as the voucher workflow and benefit review
+fields. The schema hash includes the exact ordered table manifest; adding the
+previously omitted event table changes that hash without inventing a database
+migration version.
+
+Each table is paged in complete primary-key order, including all columns of
+composite keys. Regression tests compare those keys with the actual release
+migrations and export 2,005 rows sharing the same key prefix for each composite
+table, checking that changing legal index plans cannot repeat or omit rows.
+
+Four transient tables are deliberately empty after restoration:
+`password_sessions`, `otp_challenges`, `rate_limits`, and
+`responsible_play_locks`. People must sign in again. The responsible-play lock
+row only serializes transactions and is recreated by the next operation;
+`responsible_play_profiles` and `responsible_play_deposit_reservations` remain
+in the backup, preserving limits, cooling-off/self-exclusion windows, pending
+limit changes, and outstanding reservations.
+
+The deployed 50-table 0107 manifest with schema SHA-256
+`2648becd21723884358bcaf90f5928dc19c7cc58dcf090c87ca1db3c00c34d69`
+is also accepted for recovery. Its receipt retains the old hash and uses
+`status: "verified_legacy_coverage"`, `coverageComplete: false`, and
+`missingTables: ["marketing_webhook_events"]`. Those missing records cannot be
+recreated from the old file. Such a receipt does not satisfy the current
+readiness contract. Other versions or unrecognized manifests fail closed;
+this compatibility rule does not claim support for every historical backup.
+
+## Capture consistency
+
+On Render, `RenderD1Compat.withReadSnapshot` acquires one client from the pool
+and starts `BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ READ ONLY`. Every table and
+page is read through that client. The scoped reader accepts only the allowed
+backup page SELECT form and has no write or batch method. Ordinary application
+requests keep their existing pool path; this does not start a transaction on a
+shared request connection or change session-wide isolation settings.
+
+Once the rows are collected, the exporter rolls back the read-only transaction
+and releases the client before hashing, gzip compression or RSA/AES encryption.
+The owner route writes its backup-created audit event afterwards through the
+ordinary database object. Begin/read failures still attempt rollback and discard
+the client; rollback failures request client destruction. Release failures also
+fail the export. No failure silently retries through non-snapshot reads.
+
+The encrypted payload, envelope and metadata use
+`readConsistency: "transaction_snapshot"` when the snapshot capability was used.
+Sources without that capability, including native D1 and simple test adapters,
+use `readConsistency: "unverified"`. Historical files missing this field are
+also treated as unverified. The restore tools check envelope/payload consistency
+and carry that value into their receipt; their existing restore-status and
+readiness rules are unchanged. A successful restore receipt alone is not proof
+that an unverified capture was consistent during concurrent writes.
+
+PostgreSQL repeatable read fixes the snapshot at the first non-transaction-control
+statement and reuses it for later reads. It must use one acquired client, not
+`pool.query` across clients. Native D1 `withSession` provides sequential
+consistency, which is not a substitute for this snapshot contract. See the
+[PostgreSQL isolation documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html),
+[node-postgres transaction guidance](https://node-postgres.com/features/transactions),
+and [D1 database API](https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/worker-api/d1-database/).
+
+## Run an isolated rehearsal
+
+Use an authorized encrypted backup JSON file, its matching RSA private key,
+and the private-key passphrase. Keep the file away from the application
+database and the private key/passphrase in a separate secret store. Creating
+a backup through the owner API writes an audit event; it is a separate
+authorized action from running these local verification commands.
 
